{
  "dom_id": "step-q115973",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A child on a scooter is running away from a $345\\,\\text{cm}$ tall lamppost at $4.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$ The height of the child on the scooter is $96.6\\,cm.$\nHow fast is the length of the child's shadow changing?",
  "worked_solution": "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14209554/assets/fefe072c51da577b504d81ccd1bb3546e77bca9bf9885950ca0c0c2091f9d335.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the child's distance from the lamppost, while $y$ is the length of his shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the child's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4.5\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the child's velocity is *positive* because he is running *away* from the lamppost, and therefore, the distance between the child and the lamppost *increases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{345} & =\\frac{y}{96.6} \\\\ 96.6x+96.6y & =345y \\\\ 96.6x & =248.4y \\\\ y & =\\frac{966}{2484}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{18}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{7}{18}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{18}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4.5\\,\\text{m/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{7}{18}\\cdot (4.5) \\\\  & =1.75\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the child's shadow increases at approximately $1.75\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
